WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:05.970
All these years after his accidental death
in March 1993, Brandon Lee continues to fascinate

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:07.120
fans both new and old.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.600
Son of martial arts legend Bruce Lee, the
actor is best known for his truly haunting

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.260
performance in the goth-tinged 1994 film The
Crow.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.600
"I see you've made your decision.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.160
Now let's see you enforce it."

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.620
Here's everything you need to know about Brandon
Lee.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:27.300
In 1992, Brandon Lee told Deseret News,

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.120
"I've always wanted to be an actor.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.801
From the time I was really young, it's just
what I've always wanted to do...but it was

00:00:32.801 --> 00:00:35.660
never my idea to become a martial arts action
actor."

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:40.140
From a very young age, Brandon was trained
in his father's own unique style of kung fu,

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.880
Jeet Kune Do, which incorporates a mix of
martial arts techniques.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.840
As Brandon said in a 1992 interview,

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.660
"I started training with my dad really as
soon as I could walk.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.699
I mean, my dad was a really diligent trainer."

00:00:53.699 --> 00:00:57.680
According to Entertainment Weekly, Brandon
could destroy a wooden board with a single

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.600
kick by the time he was six years old.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:04.100
Meanwhile, people were constantly training
with Bruce at home, making for quite the unusual

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:04.800
childhood.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:08.800
"When I was a little kid, a lot of my friends
didn't want to come over to the house because

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.700
there were always these men in the backyard
screaming and breaking things, you know?"

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.880
Brandon was only eight at the time of his
father's passing in 1973.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:21.640
Following Bruce's death, the surviving Lees
returned to Los Angeles, where Brandon struggled

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:22.600
to find his place.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.580
He was kicked out of two high schools and
dropped out of a third.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:30.680
After earning his GED, he went on to study
theater at Emerson College for a year before

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:34.420
attending the famous Lee Strasberg Theatre
and Film Institute in New York.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:39.080
As he started getting more deeply involved
with acting, his life began to flow like water.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.760
According to The Hollywood Reporter, Brandon
turned down the opportunity to play his dad

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:47.380
in the 1993 biopic Dragon: The Bruce Lee Story.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:48.520
As Lee explained:

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.560
"I was a little scared by the whole thing,
really.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.620
It's strange to play your own father, you
know?

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:54.640
I couldn't really wrap my mind around it."

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:55.880
That makes sense.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:59.500
If he'd played his dad, he would've had to
feign a romantic relationship with a woman

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.940
pretending to be his mom Linda…which would
have been awkward.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:10.220
Actor Jason Scott Lee ultimately went on to
play Bruce in the film...but opportunity came

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:15.760
knocking for Brandon in 1985 when he was cast
in the television film Kung Fu: The Movie,

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.250
starring alongside veteran actor David Carradine.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:20.640
On the whole, it's very dramatic stuff.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:23.180
"A coward.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:24.460
A peasant.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:25.840
Who carries vegetables!

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.080
An old woman who runs in the night from shadows."

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.320
Soon enough, he began popping up in more and
more action films.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:38.160
In 1985, Brandon Lee made his first and only
film in Hong Kong, Legacy of Rage.

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:42.660
Even though the film was in Cantonese, Hollywood
quickly took notice of the young star.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:47.280
Since he was as talented and photogenic as
his father, it wasn't long before Brandon

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.800
landed his first English-language leading
role in 1989's entirely missable Laser Mission.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:01.700
Curiously, the action film also starred acting
legend Ernest Borgnine, but it's definitely

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:04.480
not one of his most distinguished performances.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:06.040
"You're fond of birds."

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:06.940
"Yes.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:07.900
Yes.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:09.180
I have an affinity for them."

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.400
By the early '90s, Lee's star was on the rise.

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:17.180
In 1991, he starred in Showdown In Little
Tokyo, which co-starred Dolph Lundgren of

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:18.400
Rocky IV fame.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:20.560
"All right.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:21.180
Say it."

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:22.580
"Asian task force.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:23.860
You're Kenner?"

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:25.060
"Mm-hmm."

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:27.500
"Nelson sent me."

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.740
Thanks to that film, Lee was subsequently
offered a three-picture deal.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:34.960
One of those films happened to be the action
flick Rapid Fire.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:40.920
But it was his haunting and emotional performance
in Alex Proyas' The Crow that made his career,

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.960
earning him a cult following that continues
to grow to this day.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:47.740
Unfortunately, the young actor wouldn't live
long enough to enjoy that success.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.700
Brandon Lee had a reputation as a reckless
daredevil.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:55.500
As his former fiancée Eliza Hutton explained
to People in 1992:

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.840
"He's confident, intense and direct, and a
lot of people find that intimidating."

00:03:59.849 --> 00:04:04.569
In fact, Lee was so confident, he often rode
his Harley Davidson motorcycle with no helmet

00:04:04.569 --> 00:04:06.520
and his hands off the handlebars.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:07.300
He once said:

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:10.280
"If I want to put my head in a brick wall,
it's my business."

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:14.800
He even owned a Cadillac hearse, and used
to joke that it was good for taking on camping

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:15.300
trips.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:20.100
In an interview with Jay Leno in 1992, Lee
talked about coming home to find a burglar

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:20.600
in his home.

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:24.400
Instead of calling the police, he confronted
the robber, who lunged at him with a knife

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:25.240
and cut his arm.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:30.460
The two fought, and Lee finally called the
cops…after he separated the thief's shoulder

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:31.760
and broke his arm.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:35.730
"The police came and took him away, and after
he got out of the hospital, he got two years

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:37.520
for breaking and entering and attempted robbery."

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:38.650
"Eh, there you are."

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:42.860
It just goes to show that Lee was a major
risk taker, both onscreen and off.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:45.780
"You have the right to be dead."

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:51.400
He could be super-intense when making movies.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:55.880
For example, Brandon used his method acting
training to prepare for The Crow by soaking

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:57.270
in a tub full of ice.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:01.640
That way, he could feel how cold Eric Draven
must've been while lying in his grave.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:05.760
A producer berated him for this dangerous
stunt and told him to mind his health.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:10.770
Though it's hard to imagine anyone else in
the role, Brandon Lee wasn't the first choice

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:12.840
to play Eric Draven in The Crow.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:17.060
At first, the producers wanted to cast an
actual musician for the role of the murdered

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:17.860
indie rocker.

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:21.880
Names like Michael Jackson and Charlie Sexton
were kicked around before the team realized

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:24.920
there wouldn't be as much singing or performing
as originally planned.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:27.540
Even then, Lee's name wasn't at the top of
the list.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:32.470
Comic book writer James O'Barr wanted Johnny
Depp to play the dark superhero, but in the

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:36.340
end, Brandon Lee's martial arts experience,
and his smoldering good looks, won him the

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:36.840
role.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:38.240
"What are you supposed to be?

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:39.260
A clown or something?"

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:41.700
"Sometimes."

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:45.300
He was heavily involved with the film even
before shooting began.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:46.390
According to The Independent,

00:05:46.390 --> 00:05:51.540
"He also helped shape the story, convincing
[director Alex] Proyas...to remove a subplot...because

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:53.300
of its problematic Asian stereotyping."

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:58.420
According to co-star Jon Polito, conditions
on the set of The Crow were terrible.

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:01.980
Lee reportedly lost 20 pounds while filming.

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:06.010
Plus, the dark tale of revenge and murder
was mostly filmed at night in North Carolina's

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:07.660
freezing winter conditions.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:12.000
Lee was constantly getting drenched in water,
and filming was constantly interrupted to

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:12.940
fix his make-up.

00:06:13.229 --> 00:06:17.600
Lee reportedly filed a formal complaint against
production conditions mere days before his

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:18.120
death.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:23.180
In fact, things were so rough that his manager
called producers in a rage, accusing them

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:24.680
of trying to kill Lee.

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:27.020
"Creatures of the night, they never learn!"

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:31.720
During the scene in which Eric Draven crashes
through the glass door of a pawn shop, Lee

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:36.490
was cut badly by the prop glass, prompting
Polito to express concern about all the physical

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:38.240
risks his co-star was taking.

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:41.240
All sorts of on-set injuries occurred during
shooting.

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:46.120
Speaking to Inside Edition in 1993, an anonymous
crew member stopped just short of saying the

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:47.160
shoot was "haunted":

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:53.500
"Nothing ever seemed to go right....There
definitely were some bad vibes on the set."

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:57.220
One crew member reportedly drove a screwdriver
through his hand by accident.

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:01.920
Another member was severely burned after the
crane he was operating came into contact with

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:02.780
power lines.

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:07.530
A stuntman reportedly fell through the roof
of a set during rehearsal, breaking a number

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:08.540
of ribs.

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:12.920
Meanwhile, a series of hurricanes tore through
North Carolina during filming, destroying

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:14.520
a number of The Crow's sets.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:17.120
But the most tragic accident was yet to come.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:24.380
On March 31st, 1993, Brandon Lee was accidentally
shot in the abdomen by co-star Michael Massee

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:26.540
with an improperly loaded prop gun.

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:30.100
They were filming the scene in which Eric
Draven comes home to find his fiancée being

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:31.100
assaulted.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:36.180
The tip of a dummy bullet, a mock round used
in close-ups, was lodged in the barrel in

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:37.180
front of the blank.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:40.360
When Masse pulled the trigger, the blank sent
the fragment flying.

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:45.200
Tragically, it lodged in the actor's spine,
and after six hours of surgery, Brandon Lee

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:47.220
died in Wilmington, North Carolina.

00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:49.220
He was just 28 years old.

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:52.260
His death was ruled an accident by negligence.

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:56.600
Contrary to popular belief, Brandon Lee's
death is never seen in the final cut of the

00:07:56.600 --> 00:07:57.100
film.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.920
What we do see in the finished movie in a
body double.

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:04.880
Brandon Lee's mother Linda and fiancée Eliza
Hutton were both in favor of director Alex

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:06.120
Proyas finishing The Crow.

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:11.320
However, Linda filed a lawsuit against the
production company, naming 13 people at fault

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:12.860
for her son's wrongful death.

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:15.400
The lawsuit was eventually settled out of
court.

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:19.860
Sadly, Lee and Eliza Hutton were set to be
married just a couple of weeks from the day

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:20.860
he died.

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:25.140
When Brandon Lee died, he still hadn't filmed
all his scenes for the crow.

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:29.060
As we mentioned earlier, filmmakers used a
body double to finish the film.

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:33.990
In fact, it was future John Wick creator/director
Chad Stahelski who was hired as the stand-in

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:34.500
for Lee.

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:39.700
Stahelski filmed all of Lee's remaining scenes,
and Lee's face was subsequently superimposed

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:41.040
over his own in post-production.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:45.020
The famous face-painting scene was one of
the most expensive scenes to complete, since

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:48.130
Lee's face had to be digitally inserted into
the broken mirror.

00:08:49.179 --> 00:08:53.839
Seven years after his death, Brandon Lee could
be seen in a brief cameo role in the 2000

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:56.060
Swedish film Sex, Lies, and Video Violence.

00:08:56.410 --> 00:09:00.860
The film is a very low-budget, hard-to-find
horror movie satire about a young man who

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:02.580
loves watching violent films.

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:08.380
This truly bizarro movie references loads
of classic pictures, including Alien, A Clockwork

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:09.900
Orange, and RoboCop.

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:14.220
Somehow, even Mel Brooks makes a cameo…and
so does Brandon Lee.

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:19.340
In his very brief appearance, Lee plays a
befuddled pedestrian who's clearly seen some

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:21.190
very hardcore stuff go down.

00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:22.350
"He's got a gun?

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:23.730
I just saw the guy.

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:24.620
He's like over there."

00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:30.660
Before Bruce Lee died of a cerebral edema
in 1973, the martial arts star had two kids:

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:31.560
Brandon and Shannon.

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:36.480
Today, Shannon Lee is his sole remaining child,
and she's doing everything she can to preserve

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:38.900
the memories of both her father and her brother.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:44.170
She runs the Bruce Lee Foundation and oversees
movies and documentaries about her dad's life.

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:49.200
In 2018, on the 25th anniversary of Brandon
Lee's death, Shannon appeared on the Bruce

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.100
Lee Podcast to talk about her beloved big
brother.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:55.560
She even shared some of Brandon's journal
entries for the first time.

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:59.240
During the podcast, Shannon talked about how
Brandon would often prank her, but when it

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:02.940
came down to it, he was her fiercest defender.

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:07.560
Shannon recalls the time her aunt tried to
force her to stop using her comfort blanket

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:09.020
just after Bruce Lee's death.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:13.360
"Brandon just, like, stood up for me.

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:18.980
And he, like, put his hands on his hips and
was yelling."

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:22.410
Long story short: Brandon triumphantly got
Shannon's blankie back.

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:23.960
What a great brother.

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:26.960
Shannon also talked about Brandon's love of
reading and books.

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:31.260
He was apparently quite fond of curling up
with a dictionary to look up words he didn't

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:31.760
know.

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:35.980
"He knew the definition of everything."

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:37.690
Brandon was a born showman, too.

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:42.020
As a kid, he'd write down episodes of The
Twilight Zone by hand and perform them at

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:42.520
school.

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:46.880
Sadly, Shannon was supposed to be the best
man at her brother's wedding, but that never

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:47.880
came to pass.

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:52.120
Brandon Lee is still loved and missed by his
family, friends, and fans.

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:56.500
Buried next to his father in Seattle's Lake
View Cemetery, the site has become a shrine

00:10:56.500 --> 00:10:59.940
to both legends, and it's visited by fans
from around the world.

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:04.940
People leave flowers, notes, and other memorabilia
to pay their respects to the father and son

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:07.660
who touched so many peoples' lives through
their work.

00:11:08.260 --> 00:11:12.439
Reflecting on The Crow twenty-five years later,
The Saturday Evening Post wrote that,

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:14.620
"[The film] captured the angst of a generation."

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:18.420
As Roger Ebert wrote in his 1994 review of
the film:

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:23.160
“It is a sad irony that this film is not
only the best thing [Brandon Lee] accomplished,

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:27.000
but is actually more of a screen achievement
than any of the films of his father, Bruce

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:27.500
Lee.”

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:31.680
Every time someone watches The Crow, they
help ensure that Brandon's unique legacy lives

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:32.180
on.

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:34.120
"It can't rain all the time."

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:36.160
"Eric?"

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:38.760
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:42.160
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
movies are coming soon.

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:46.240
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

